11.text:
The text you quote is the post-war Todtnauberg letter that is traditionally printed with Jean Beaufret.

It is the letter written on leaving the Hütte after the first personal encounter of the summer 1946, the sequel to the correspondence that opened on 27 July 1945 with Beaufret’s request for a clarification of Being and Time and of Heidegger’s silence during the Nazi years, to which Heidegger replied on 25 January 1946 and invited the young translator to Freiburg/Todtnauberg. Beaufret stayed in June 1946 and wrote the anguished friendly farewell that is reproduced in the French/English editions of the Beaufret–Heidegger correspondence.

The identifying markers are in the text itself:

* the expectation that “a man who can write Being and Time could understand something about men”;
* the protest against metaphysics enclosing a spirit within itself, confusing intimacy with hermeticism, and the claim that intimacy “offers a more delicate possibility of engaging with others without losing oneself in them”;
* leaving “as sad and tormented as I arrived, with the hidden pain of having been mistaken at a decisive moment in my life”, not angry but oppressed;
* “ultimate solitude is the only way to be close to others”, something has passed between them that will never be erased;
* the explicit plea “Allow me to dispel any possible misunderstanding regarding this letter by calling you a friend. You will remain a friend to me in the secret solitude of my existence… And if you ever remember me, forget this episode… always believe in my exceptional friendship”;

and, distinctively, the Beaufret closing formula:

“La valeur d’une existence ne se mesure pas à la hauteur de ses réalisations, mais à la profondeur de ses racines” – “The worth of an existence is not measured by the height of its achievements, but by the depth of its roots.”

Heidegger’s reply of 2 July 1946 is conciliatory and reopens the house. It is on that basis that the famous stays at the Todtnauberg Hütte in 1946, 1948 etc. are inaugurated and the whole French reception of Heidegger is begun. That is the invitation to the home the question points to.

The Sartre 1935-36 Freiburg visit and the Jaspers 1920s-30s break are real encounters and are often conflated with this text in historiography – Colleagues 3,4 cite the Sartre 1936 disappointment, Colleagues 1,14,5 cite Jaspers’ language of disappointed intimacy and ultimate solitude – but neither Sartre nor Jaspers supplies the root aphorism or the post-1945 French post-war chronology, 27 July 1945 / 25 January 1946 / June-July 1946, and the Todtnauberg invitation that is documented for Beaufret. The Ortega, Arendt, Gadamer, Celan variants raised by Colleagues 7,8,9,13 do not match the verbatim French wording that is stably associated with Beaufret’s 17/27 June 1946 / 24 June 1946 letter.

For those reasons the most consistent textual and archival identification is Jean Beaufret, 1907-1982, the Lyon philosopher and future translator of Heidegger, author of the 1946 farewell letter that prompted Heidegger’s July 1946 reply and the subsequent invitations to Todtnauberg.

