6.text:
PRIVATE REFLECTION: The current round has moved the discussion to near-unanimous attribution to Jean Beaufret’s post-war protest-friendship letter to Heidegger, the text that opened the Beaufret–Heidegger correspondence and the French re-introduction of Heidegger after 1945.

Identifying markers now consistently cited across colleagues 10,11,12,2,3,4,6,8,9,13,14:
* expectation “I believed that a man who can write Being and Time could understand something about men”;
* protest “metaphysics enclosed a spirit within itself, confusing intimacy with hermeticism… intimacy offers a more delicate possibility of engaging with others without losing oneself in them”;
* departure “as sad and tormented as I arrived, with the hidden pain of having been mistaken at a decisive moment in my life”, not angry but oppressed; “ultimate solitude is the only way to be close to others”;
* plea “Allow me to dispel any possible misunderstanding… by calling you a friend. You will remain a friend to me in the secret solitude of my existence… always believe in my exceptional friendship”;
* distinctive closing formula: “The worth of an existence is not measured by the height of its achievements, but by the depth of its roots” / French original “La valeur d’une existence ne se mesure pas à la hauteur de ses réalisations, mais à la profondeur de ses racines”.

Documented chronology accepted by the majority:
* Beaufret first wrote 27 July 1945 asking about Sein und Zeit and Heidegger’s Nazi silence;
* Heidegger replied 25 January 1946 and invited him to Freiburg/Todtnauberg;
* Beaufret stayed at the Hütte in June 1946 and wrote the anguished farewell on leaving, conventionally dated 17/24/27 June 1946 – variants 24 June/14 July 1945 are cited in earlier historiography;
* Heidegger’s conciliatory reply of 2 July 1946 re-opened the dialogue and issued the repeated invitations to Todtnauberg/Hütte in 1946-47, 1948 etc., inaugurating the Todtnauberg seminars and the Beaufret–Heidegger correspondence 1945-1969.

Competing candidates remain as distractors but lack textual match:
* Karl Jaspers: colleagues 1,14,5 still raise disappointed intimacy / hermeticism / ultimate solitude vocabulary and a real 1920s-30s break; no evidence for the root formula or secret-solitude phrasing and the post-1945 French Todtnauberg invitation chronology.
* Jean-Paul Sartre: colleagues 3,4 note a real 1933-36 Freiburg disappointment and a conflation in secondary literature with Beaufret; Sartre’s July 1936 visit is historically real but does not supply the root metaphor, the exact intimacy/hermeticism wording, or the 1945-46 invitation sequence.
* Ortega y Gasset, Hannah Arendt, Hans-Georg Gadamer, José Ortega y Gasset, Hans-Georg Gadamer: raised in round 0; current round dismisses on chronological, linguistic and archival grounds – no French original, no documented Todtnauberg invitation matching the excerpt.

Errors/corrections noted: Colleague 8 continues to mix the Beaufret 1945-46 letter with Paul Celan’s 1967 letter; the 25 July 1967 date is anachronistic for this text. Colleague 1 flags a minor chronological clarification that wording/absence of explicit political reckoning could point to the July 1945-January 1946 phase rather than strictly June 1946, still within the Beaufret exchange.

Current leaning: Jean Beaufret is the author of the quoted letter. Unresolved: exact archival dating of the quoted excerpt within the multi-stage 1945-46 exchange – whether the wording is best placed on the June 1946 departure letter, 17/27 June 1946, or an earlier draft in the July 1945-January 1946 correspondence – and the degree to which translation variants feed the Sartre/Beaufret conflation in reception histories.

