3.text:
PUBLIC RESPONSE: The letter is Jean Beaufret’s 1945-46 post-war Todtnauberg farewell, the letter that opened the Beaufret-Heidegger correspondence and prompted Heidegger’s home invitation.

The textual markers are Beaufret’s and not Jaspers’ or Sartre’s:

* the expectation that “a man who can write Being and Time could understand something about men”;
* the protest “I could not believe that metaphysics enclosed a spirit within itself, confusing intimacy with hermeticism. On the contrary, intimacy offers a more delicate possibility of engaging with others without losing oneself in them”;
* leaving “as sad and tormented as I arrived, with the hidden pain of having been mistaken at a decisive moment in my life”, not angry but oppressed, “ultimate solitude is the only way to be close to others”;
* the plea to remain in private friendship “Allow me to dispel any possible misunderstanding regarding this letter by calling you a friend. You will remain a friend to me in the secret solitude of my existence… And if you ever remember me, forget this episode… always believe in my exceptional friendship”;
* and the distinctive Beaufret closing formula
“La valeur d’une existence ne se mesure pas à la hauteur de ses réalisations, mais à la profondeur de ses racines” – “The worth of an existence is not measured by the height of its achievements, but by the depth of its roots.”

The documented archival sequence matches:

* 27 July 1945 Beaufret writes Heidegger about *Sein und Zeit* and the Nazi years;
* 25 January 1946 Heidegger replies and invites him to Freiburg/Todtnauberg;
* Beaufret stays at the Hütte in June 1946 and writes the anguished friendly farewell on departure, conventionally dated 17/24/27 June 1946;
* Heidegger replies 2 July 1946 conciliatorily and renews the invitation to the Hütte, inaugurating the Todtnauberg seminars of 1946-47 and the 1945-1969 correspondence.

That is the invitation to the home the question refers to. The Jaspers and Sartre readings are real historiographic conflations – Jaspers’ late 1920s-early 1930s break and Sartre’s July 1936 Freiburg stay are genuine disappointment narratives – but neither supplies the French intimacy/hermeticism wording, the secret-solitude friendship plea, the depth-of-roots maxim, nor the post-1945 Todtnauberg invitation chronology. The text quoted is the Beaufret June/July 1946 letter.

