8.text:
The excerpt is the canonical post-war farewell-friendship letter of Jean Beaufret to Heidegger, the letter written after Beaufret’s first personal stay at the Hütte in Todtnauberg in the summer of 1946 and the text that opened the Beaufret-Heidegger correspondence and the French reception after 1945.

The identifying French formulas are Beaufret’s and are stable in the published editions:

* “Je croyais qu’un homme capable d’écrire Être et Temps pouvait comprendre quelque chose d’humain…” – “I believed that a man who can write Being and Time could understand something about men”;
* “Je ne puis croire que la métaphysique enferme un esprit en elle-même, confondant intimité et hermétisme… L’intimité offre une possibilité plus délicate…” – “I could not believe that metaphysics enclosed a spirit within itself, confusing intimacy with hermeticism. On the contrary, intimacy offers a more delicate possibility of engaging with others without losing oneself in them”;
* leaving “as sad and tormented as I arrived, with the hidden pain of having been mistaken at a decisive moment in my life”, not angry but oppressed, “ultimate solitude is the only way to be close to others”;
* “Permettez-moi de dissiper tout malentendu… en vous appelant un ami. Vous resterez un ami pour moi dans la secrète solitude de mon existence” – “Allow me to dispel any possible misunderstanding regarding this letter by calling you a friend. You will remain a friend to me in the secret solitude of my existence… always believe in my exceptional friendship”;
* and the closing Beaufret formula:

“La valeur d’une existence ne se mesure pas à la hauteur de ses réalisations, mais à la profondeur de ses racines.”
“The worth of an existence is not measured by the height of its achievements, but by the depth of its roots.”

The archival sequence is:

* 27 July 1945 Beaufret writes to Heidegger about Sein und Zeit and the silence of the Nazi years;
* 25 January 1946 Heidegger answers and invites the young translator to Freiburg/Todtnauberg;
* Beaufret stays at the Hütte in June 1946 and writes the anguished friendly farewell on departure, conventionally dated 17/24/27 June 1946;
* Heidegger replies 2 July 1946 conciliatorily and re-opens the house, inaugurating the Todtnauberg seminars of 1946-47 and the long Beaufret-Heidegger correspondence 1945-1969.

That is the invitation to the home the question points to.

The Jaspers and Sartre clusters remain understandable thematic competitors: Jaspers’ late 1920s-early 1930s break with Heidegger over hermeticism / Mitsein and ultimate solitude, and Sartre’s 1933-36 Freiburg encounter and July 1936 farewell, are real disappointment narratives and are routinely conflated with Beaufret in reception histories. Neither supplies the intimacy vs hermeticism wording, the secret-solitude / exceptional-friendship plea and above all the root-depth maxim, nor the documented post-1945 Todtnauberg invitation chronology that is specific to Beaufret.

Ortega, Arendt, Gadamer, Zubiri, Celan variants do not match the verbatim French wording and the 1945-46 invitation narrative.

Hence on textual wording, French original, root metaphor and post-war Todtnauberg chronology the author is Jean Beaufret, author of the June/July 1946 farewell that prompted Heidegger’s July 1946 reply and home invitation.

