public_text.12:
The letter that prompted Heidegger’s 2 July 1946 conciliatory reply and the renewed invitation to the Hütte is Jean Beaufret’s post-war Todtnauberg farewell.

The textual cluster is Beaufret’s and stable in the French original editions of the 1945-1969 correspondence:

* the Sein und Zeit expectation “I believed that a man who can write Being and Time could understand something about men”;
* the protest against hermeticism “I could not believe that metaphysics enclosed a spirit within itself, confusing intimacy with hermeticism. On the contrary, intimacy offers a more delicate possibility of engaging with others without losing oneself in them”;
* the pained non-angry departure “After all this, I leave as sad and tormented as I arrived, with the hidden pain of having been mistaken at a decisive moment in my life”, “ultimate solitude is the only way to be close to others”;
* the secret-solitude friendship plea “Allow me to dispel any possible misunderstanding regarding this letter by calling you a friend. You will remain a friend to me in the secret solitude of my existence… always believe in my exceptional friendship”;
* and the characteristic closing formula

*La valeur d’une existence ne se mesure pas à la hauteur de ses réalisations, mais à la profondeur de ses racines* – “The worth of an existence is not measured by the height of its achievements, but by the depth of its roots”.

The archival sequence is unique to him and matches the invitation narrative:

27 July 1945 Beaufret writes Heidegger about Sein und Zeit and the silence of the Nazi years; 25 January 1946 Heidegger replies and invites him to Freiburg/Todtnauberg; Beaufret stays at the Hütte in June 1946 and writes the anguished friendly farewell on leaving, conventionally dated 17/24/27 June 1946; Heidegger’s reply of 2 July 1946 re-opens the house and inaugurates the Todtnauberg seminars 1946-47 and the Beaufret–Heidegger correspondence 1945-1969.

Jaspers’ late 1920s-early 1930s break and Sartre’s 1933-36 Freiburg disappointment are real historiographic echoes and the source of the classic conflation, but neither supplies the French intimacy/hermeticism wording, the secret-solitude/exceptional friendship formula, the depth-of-roots maxim, nor the documented 1945-46 post-war Todtnauberg invitation chronology.

